DEF_ADD([
{
  id: "minimal-facts",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "The Minimal Facts Argument",
  who: "Gary Habermas & Michael Licona, The Case for the Resurrection of Jesus (2004); Habermas's surveys of scholarship; William Lane Craig's 'four facts'",
  claim: `Arguing only from facts that are granted by the vast majority of scholars, including skeptics: (1) Jesus died by crucifixion; (2) his disciples sincerely believed he appeared to them risen; (3) Paul, a persecutor of the church, converted after an experience he believed was an appearance; (4) James, Jesus' skeptical brother, converted; (5) the tomb was found empty (accepted by about 75% of scholars). No naturalistic theory explains all these facts. Swoon, theft, wrong tomb, and hallucination each fail. The best explanation is that God raised Jesus from the dead.`,
  short: `The facts that are actually agreed on are much thinner than the argument needs. Scholars agree that followers *had experiences they interpreted* as appearances of the risen Jesus, not that Jesus appeared. That is just what bereavement visions, which are very common, plus religious interpretation would produce. The empty tomb is genuinely disputed, and even its defenders like Dale Allison (a Christian) concede it can't establish a resurrection. The "consensus" is a count of publications in a field dominated by Christian scholars, with an unpublished methodology. And the argument compares "God raised Jesus" against single natural theories one at a time instead of against the natural explanation that combines them.`,
  points: [
    { h: "What is actually agreed", t: `Most critical scholars agree that Jesus was crucified, and that some of his followers came to believe soon afterward that he had been raised and had appeared to them. That's a fact about *beliefs* and *experiences*. Ehrman, Sanders, Lüdemann, and others accept it while explaining the experiences as visions. The minimal facts approach turns "the disciples had experiences they believed were appearances" into "the appearances need a supernatural explanation," which is the conclusion, not a fact.` },
    { h: "The 'consensus' is shaky", t: `Habermas's figures come from his own survey of publications on the resurrection, the details of which have not been published for scrutiny. Most of the literature on the resurrection is written by Christian scholars, many of them at confessional institutions, so the sample is not neutral. And historians' agreement that the disciples *believed* something is compatible with naturalistic explanations; many of the scholars counted hold exactly those explanations.` },
    { h: "Bereavement visions are common", t: `Studies of bereaved people consistently find that a large proportion, around 30–60% depending on the study, report sensing, seeing, hearing, or even being touched by the dead person (Rees, *BMJ*, 1971; Castelnovo et al., 2015). These experiences can be vivid and life-changing. A group of grieving followers who had expected their leader to bring God's kingdom, in a culture that already believed in resurrection and visions, is exactly where such experiences would be expected and interpreted as divine (see [[appearances]]).` },
    { h: "Divide and conquer", t: `Apologists refute natural theories one at a time ("hallucinations don't explain the empty tomb," "theft doesn't explain the appearances"). Historical explanations combine. Grief visions to Peter and others, interpreted through Jewish martyr-vindication beliefs and scriptures like Hosea 6:2, spread by social contagion; the tomb story arose or grew later; Paul had his own vision; and James, a family member, took a leading role. Each element uses well-attested human processes. The resurrection hypothesis requires a miracle with no independent support.` },
    { h: "The prior probability is decisive", t: `Billions of people have died; no case of resurrection after three days is independently documented. Reports of resurrections, apparitions of the dead, and miracles are, by comparison, extremely common in every culture. Even a very good historical record would face this prior. A record consisting of anonymous texts written decades later, by believers, with legendary growth, doesn't come close to overcoming it (see [[bayes-resurrection]]).` },
    { h: "Even Christian historians are cautious", t: `Dale Allison, a leading New Testament scholar and a believing Christian, concludes in *The Resurrection of Jesus: Apologetics, Polemics, History* (2021) that the evidence is consistent with both Christian and naturalistic interpretations, and that historical method alone can't settle the matter in favor of the resurrection. He leans slightly toward the empty tomb being historical, but documents many parallels to the appearance reports in visions of the dead, including collective ones.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Hallucinations can't explain group appearances. Groups don't share the same hallucination.", a: `Group experiences of apparitions are well documented, from Marian apparitions witnessed by crowds to collective sightings of the recently dead, which Allison catalogs. They don't require identical private images. They involve shared expectation, one person's report shaping others', and a group that reinterprets ambiguous experiences together. And our only "group appearance" reports come from secondhand creedal lists and later Gospels, which don't describe what anyone actually saw.`, k: "groups don't hallucinate group hallucination collective impossible same hallucination" },
    { q: "Even skeptical scholars accept these facts.", a: `They accept that Jesus died and that his followers had experiences they believed were appearances. They don't accept that he appeared. Using their agreement on the first to support a conclusion they reject is misleading. Ehrman, Lüdemann, Crossan, and Allison all accept the "minimal facts" in some form and still don't conclude that a resurrection happened.`, k: "even skeptical scholars accept facts ehrman agrees consensus" },
    { q: "What's your explanation then? You need a better theory.", a: `Grief-driven visions (starting with Peter), spread through a close-knit group, interpreted with existing Jewish beliefs about the vindication of martyrs, and followed by the development of an empty-tomb narrative. Every element is attested in human experience. And even if I had no specific explanation, "we don't know exactly what happened 2,000 years ago" is far more probable than "a man rose from the dead." Not knowing how a magician did a trick isn't evidence of real magic.`, k: "what's your explanation better theory alternative naturalistic explain facts" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Habermas, G. & Licona, M. (2004). The Case for the Resurrection of Jesus. Kregel.",
    "Allison, D. C. (2021). The Resurrection of Jesus: Apologetics, Polemics, History. T&T Clark.",
    "Allison, D. C. (2005). Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters. T&T Clark.",
    "Lüdemann, G. (2004). The Resurrection of Christ: A Historical Inquiry. Prometheus.",
    "Ehrman, B. (2014). How Jesus Became God, ch. 4–5.",
    "Rees, W. D. (1971). 'The Hallucinations of Widowhood.' British Medical Journal 4: 37–41.",
    "Castelnovo, A. et al. (2015). 'Post-bereavement hallucinatory experiences: A critical overview of population and clinical studies.' Journal of Affective Disorders 186: 266–274.",
    "Komarnitsky, K. (2014). Doubting Jesus' Resurrection: What Happened in the Black Box?, 2nd ed. Stone Arrow."
  ],
  keys: "minimal facts resurrection habermas licona historical facts scholars agree crucifixion appearances empty tomb paul james best explanation evidence for resurrection",
  rel: ["empty-tomb", "appearances", "bayes-resurrection", "alternative-theories", "legendary-growth"]
},
{
  id: "empty-tomb",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "The Tomb Was Empty",
  who: "William Lane Craig; N. T. Wright, The Resurrection of the Son of God (2003); Habermas & Licona",
  claim: `Jesus was buried by Joseph of Arimathea in a known tomb, and the tomb was found empty on Sunday morning by women. The disciples couldn't have preached the resurrection in Jerusalem if the body were still in the tomb; the authorities would simply have produced it. The Jewish authorities' own response, that the disciples stole the body (Matthew 28:11–15), admits the tomb was empty.`,
  short: `Our earliest source, Paul, never mentions an empty tomb. The story first appears in Mark, around 40 years later, and Mark ends with the women telling no one, which would explain why no one had heard of it. Romans often left crucified victims unburied or in common graves. The "stolen body" polemic is known only from Matthew's own apologetic account, written around 80–90. Even if the tomb was empty, ordinary explanations (the body moved, reburied, or stolen; a wrong tomb) are vastly more probable than resurrection. By the time the disciples preached in Jerusalem (Acts 2 says 50 days later), a body would be unrecognizable.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Paul's silence", t: `Paul's letters, written in the 50s, are the earliest Christian documents. He discusses the resurrection at length in 1 Corinthians 15, against people who doubted resurrection, and never mentions an empty tomb, the women, or a burial place. The creed he quotes says Jesus "was buried" and "was raised," which a Jew would affirm without having seen an empty tomb. His theology of a "spiritual body" (15:44) and "flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God" (15:50) doesn't require the corpse to have left.` },
    { h: "Mark's ending", t: `Mark, the first Gospel to mention the tomb, ends at 16:8: the women "went out and fled from the tomb, for terror and amazement had seized them; and they said nothing to anyone, for they were afraid." Many scholars read this as Mark's explanation for why the story was not widely known before his Gospel. The later endings (16:9–20) were added by scribes.` },
    { h: "Roman crucifixion practice", t: `Crucifixion was designed to humiliate, and leaving bodies to decay or be eaten by animals was part of the punishment. Ancient writers refer to this (Horace, *Epistles* 1.16.48; Petronius, *Satyricon* 111–112; Juvenal 14.77). Bodies were sometimes released to families as a favor (Philo, *Flaccus* 83, on holidays), and Jewish law required burial before sunset (Deuteronomy 21:22–23; Josephus, *Jewish War* 4.317). The heel bone of a crucified man named Jehohanan, found in a family ossuary in 1968, shows that at least one crucified man was buried. So burial is possible, but it isn't guaranteed, and many scholars (Crossan; Ehrman, 2014; Cook, 2014) doubt it for a man condemned for sedition.` },
    { h: "Joseph of Arimathea and the guard", t: `Mark says Joseph was a member of the council (15:43), the same council of which Mark said "all of them condemned him" (14:64). Later Gospels resolve the tension by making Joseph a secret disciple (Matthew 27:57; John 19:38). The guard at the tomb appears only in Matthew. It's placed there the day *after* the burial (27:62), leaving a night unguarded, and serves to answer the charge that the disciples stole the body, which suggests it was created as an apologetic response.` },
    { h: "Disappearing bodies were a literary type", t: `Richard C. Miller (*Resurrection and Reception in Early Christianity*, 2015) shows that Greek and Roman literature has a common "translation fable" pattern, in which a hero's body vanishes and he's taken up to the gods: Romulus (Livy 1.16; Plutarch, *Romulus* 27–28), Heracles, Aristeas (Herodotus 4.14–15), and Alcmene, whose body was replaced by a stone in her coffin. Audiences recognized a missing body as a sign of divine status. Mark's empty tomb fits this pattern.` },
    { h: "If it was empty, so what?", t: `Empty tombs have ordinary explanations: the body was moved (by Joseph to a permanent grave, or by authorities), stolen (tomb robbery was common enough that an imperial edict of the period, the so-called Nazareth Inscription, threatened death for it), or the women went to the wrong tomb. Each of these is improbable but has a known precedent. Resurrection has none. The disciples' claim 50 days later couldn't have been refuted by a body in the advanced decomposition of a Jerusalem spring. And the earliest preaching emphasized appearances, not a tomb.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "The authorities would have produced the body to stop the preaching.", a: `According to Acts 2, public preaching began at Pentecost, seven weeks after the crucifixion. A body by then would be decomposed beyond recognition. The authorities had little reason to care about a small sect in its first weeks. If the body was in a common grave, it couldn't be identified at all. And the disciples' message, as the earliest sources show, was about appearances, not a tomb. People who believe in a "spiritual body" wouldn't be dissuaded by a corpse.`, k: "authorities produce body stop preaching jerusalem disprove" },
    { q: "The Jewish leaders said the disciples stole the body, so they admitted the tomb was empty.", a: `That "admission" is reported only by Matthew, a Christian writing around 80–90 CE, decades later, in a story invented to rebut a later Jewish argument. It tells us what Matthew's opponents said in Matthew's time, in response to Christian claims. It isn't independent evidence from 30 CE.`, k: "jewish leaders stole the body admitted empty tomb matthew 28 guard" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Allison, D. C. (2021). The Resurrection of Jesus. T&T Clark.",
    "Cook, J. G. (2014). Crucifixion in the Mediterranean World. Mohr Siebeck.",
    "Crossan, J. D. (1994). Jesus: A Revolutionary Biography. HarperSanFrancisco, ch. 6.",
    "Ehrman, B. (2014). How Jesus Became God, ch. 4.",
    "Miller, R. C. (2015). Resurrection and Reception in Early Christianity. Routledge.",
    "Magness, J. (2005). 'Ossuaries and the Burials of Jesus and James.' Journal of Biblical Literature 124(1): 121–154. (argues burial plausible)",
    "Craig, W. L. (1989). Assessing the New Testament Evidence for the Historicity of the Resurrection of Jesus. Edwin Mellen.",
    "Tzaferis, V. (1970). 'Jewish Tombs at and near Giv'at ha-Mivtar, Jerusalem.' Israel Exploration Journal 20: 18–32."
  ],
  keys: "empty tomb tomb was empty joseph of arimathea women found tomb empty body stolen guards authorities produce body burial roman crucifixion",
  rel: ["minimal-facts", "women-witnesses", "legendary-growth", "alternative-theories"]
},
{
  id: "appearances",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "The Resurrection Appearances Can't Be Explained Naturally",
  who: "William Lane Craig; Gary Habermas; N. T. Wright",
  claim: `Jesus appeared to many people, on many occasions, individually and in groups, to believers and skeptics, indoors and outdoors. He was touched, ate food, and talked at length. Hallucinations are private, rare, and don't transform people. Something objective happened.`,
  short: `The earliest evidence, Paul's list in 1 Corinthians 15, uses the same word ("he appeared," *ōphthē*) for the other appearances as for Paul's own, which was a vision. It gives no physical details. The physical, touchable Jesus who eats fish appears only in the later Gospels (Luke and John), in scenes that answer skeptics' doubts. Visions of the dead are among the most common human experiences, especially in grief. Collective visions are documented. And visions have transformed people in every religion.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Paul's list is the earliest evidence", t: `"He appeared (*ōphthē*) to Cephas, then to the twelve. Then he appeared to more than five hundred... Then he appeared to James, then to all the apostles. Last of all... he appeared also to me" (1 Corinthians 15:5–8). Paul puts his own experience in the same series, with the same verb. Paul's experience was a vision: Acts describes a light and a voice, with his companions seeing no one (9:7) and Paul calling it a "heavenly vision" (26:19). Paul says God "revealed his Son *in me*" (Galatians 1:16).` },
    { h: "The appearances get more physical over time", t: `In Mark (c. 70), there are no appearances, only the promise of one in Galilee. In Matthew (c. 80–90), Jesus appears briefly, and "some doubted" (28:17). In Luke (c. 85–95), he insists "a ghost does not have flesh and bones as you see that I have" and eats broiled fish (24:39–43). In John (c. 90–110), Thomas is invited to put his finger in the wounds (20:27). The movement from vision to touchable body follows the needs of later apologetics against skeptics and docetists (who said Jesus only seemed to have a body).` },
    { h: "Grief visions are common and convincing", t: `Studies of bereavement find that many people, roughly 30–60% depending on the study, experience the presence of the dead: seeing them, hearing them, feeling their touch, or having conversations (Rees, 1971; Castelnovo et al., 2015). People typically find these experiences real and comforting. Peter, who had denied Jesus, and the other disciples, who had fled, would have been in intense grief and guilt, a setting where such experiences are especially likely.` },
    { h: "Collective experiences happen", t: `Dale Allison (2005, 2021) documents reports of collective apparitions of the dead and of religious figures. Crowds have reported seeing the Virgin Mary (Knock, Ireland, 1879; Zeitoun, Egypt, 1968–70; Medjugorje, from 1981), and at Fatima in 1917 tens of thousands reported the sun dancing (see [[fatima]]). Group experiences arise through shared expectation, suggestion, and one person interpreting an ambiguous perception for the others. They don't need a real object.` },
    { h: "Visions transform people everywhere", t: `Muhammad's visions of Gabriel founded Islam. Joseph Smith's visions founded Mormonism, and his witnesses never recanted. Visions and conversions are common in Hindu, Buddhist, and shamanic traditions. That transformative experiences occurred tells us about human psychology, not about which religion is true.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Hallucinations are private. 500 people can't share the same one.", a: `The "500" is a single unsupported line in Paul (see [[five-hundred]]). Collective visionary experiences, where a group reports seeing something no outside observer could detect, are well documented, from Marian apparitions to Fatima. They don't require identical private images; social influence and reinterpretation do the work. We also have no first-hand account from any member of a group appearance.`, k: "hallucinations private 500 share same hallucination group" },
    { q: "Jesus ate fish and was touched. A vision can't do that.", a: `Those details appear only in Luke and John, written 50 to 70 years later, in scenes that answer doubts about whether Jesus was a ghost. They're the end point of a development, not the earliest testimony. Paul, the only eyewitness to an appearance who wrote anything, describes a vision and a "spiritual body."`, k: "ate fish touched wounds physical body thomas flesh and bones" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Allison, D. C. (2005). Resurrecting Jesus. T&T Clark.",
    "Allison, D. C. (2021). The Resurrection of Jesus. T&T Clark.",
    "Rees, W. D. (1971). 'The Hallucinations of Widowhood.' British Medical Journal 4: 37–41.",
    "Castelnovo, A. et al. (2015). 'Post-bereavement hallucinatory experiences.' Journal of Affective Disorders 186: 266–274.",
    "Lüdemann, G. (2004). The Resurrection of Christ. Prometheus.",
    "Goulder, M. (1996). 'The Baseless Fabric of a Vision.' In D'Costa (ed.), Resurrection Reconsidered. Oneworld."
  ],
  keys: "resurrection appearances appeared hallucination visions grief bereavement group appearances physical body ate fish touched wounds thomas ophthe",
  rel: ["five-hundred", "paul-conversion", "minimal-facts", "legendary-growth", "fatima"]
},
{
  id: "five-hundred",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "Jesus Appeared to Five Hundred Witnesses",
  who: "1 Corinthians 15:6; popular apologetics",
  claim: `Paul says Jesus "appeared to more than five hundred brothers and sisters at one time, most of whom are still alive" (1 Corinthians 15:6). Paul is inviting his readers to check with the living witnesses. No one would make such a claim if it could be easily refuted.`,
  short: `This is one sentence in one source, with no names, no place, no time, and no description. No other early Christian writing mentions it, not even Acts, which loves crowd scenes, or any Gospel. The Corinthians, hundreds of miles away in Greece, had no way to find and question unnamed witnesses in Palestine. It's a claim *about* witnesses, not testimony *from* them.`,
  points: [
    { h: "No details", t: `Paul doesn't say where, when, or what the 500 saw. He doesn't name any of them. For an event that would be the most powerful evidence in the whole case, the silence of every other source is striking. The Gospels describe appearances to small groups; none mentions 500 people.` },
    { h: "Not checkable", t: `"Most of whom are still alive" sounds like an invitation to verify, but without names or places, verification was impossible for Corinthian readers in the 50s. There's no evidence that anyone did check, and no record of any of the 500 telling their story.` },
    { h: "Possibly a Pentecost tradition", t: `Some scholars suggest that the 500 tradition reflects an ecstatic group experience like the one Acts 2 describes at Pentecost (tongues of fire, speaking in tongues), later counted as a resurrection appearance. If so, it was a collective religious experience of the kind seen in charismatic revivals, not a visual meeting with a resurrected body.` },
    { h: "Paul's own reliability", t: `Paul is passing on a tradition he received, not reporting his own observation. He wasn't among the 500. Even if he reports the tradition accurately, it's hearsay about an unspecified experience. Compare the 1830 Book of Mormon, which includes signed testimony from three named witnesses and eight more who said they saw the golden plates, and none of the eleven ever publicly retracted it. Christians don't find that convincing.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Paul says most are still alive. He's inviting people to check.", a: `Check whom, and where? Paul names none of them and gives no place. A Corinthian couldn't travel to Palestine and find "the 500." It's a rhetorical flourish, and it's not unique: the Book of Mormon includes eleven *named* witnesses who signed statements, and no Christian accepts their testimony.`, k: "most still alive inviting to check paul verify 500" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Lüdemann, G. (2004). The Resurrection of Christ, ch. on 1 Cor 15:6.",
    "Allison, D. C. (2021). The Resurrection of Jesus. T&T Clark.",
    "Price, R. M. (2005). 'Apocryphal Apparitions: 1 Corinthians 15:3–11 as a Post-Pauline Interpolation.' In Price & Lowder (eds.), The Empty Tomb. Prometheus. (a minority view)",
    "Fitzmyer, J. (2008). First Corinthians. Anchor Yale Bible. Yale University Press."
  ],
  keys: "500 witnesses five hundred brethren at once 1 corinthians 15:6 most still alive check eyewitnesses",
  rel: ["appearances", "creed-1cor15", "minimal-facts"]
},
{
  id: "martyrdom",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "The Apostles Died for Their Testimony",
  who: "Josh McDowell; Sean McDowell, The Fate of the Apostles (2015; rev. 2024); popular apologetics ('people don't die for what they know is a lie')",
  claim: `The apostles were tortured and martyred for proclaiming that they had seen the risen Jesus. People may die for a lie they believe is true, but no one dies for something they *know* is a lie. The apostles were in a position to know whether they saw Jesus, so their deaths prove their sincerity, and sincere eyewitnesses to the resurrection can't all be mistaken.`,
  short: `No one has to claim the apostles lied. The skeptical view is that they sincerely believed, on the basis of visionary experiences. Martyrdom proves sincerity, not truth: sincere people die for false beliefs in every religion. And the evidence that most of the apostles were martyred at all is weak. Even Sean McDowell, whose doctoral research was on this question, concludes that good evidence exists only for a few (Peter, Paul, James son of Zebedee, James the brother of Jesus), with most accounts coming from legendary texts written centuries later. None of the sources says they were offered the chance to recant the resurrection specifically.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Sincerity is not in dispute", t: `The "lie" framing is a straw man. Critical scholars think the disciples sincerely believed Jesus had been raised, because of experiences they had. The question is what caused those experiences. Dying for a belief shows you hold it. It doesn't show the belief is true.` },
    { h: "Weak evidence for most martyrdoms", t: `The traditions about the deaths of Andrew, Thomas, Bartholomew, Matthew, and others come mostly from apocryphal Acts written in the 2nd–4th centuries, full of fantastic elements (talking animals, miraculous rescues). Sean McDowell's study grades most of them as possible or plausible at best. 1 Clement 5 (c. 96 CE) mentions that Peter and Paul suffered, without details. Acts 12:2 reports James son of Zebedee killed by Herod Agrippa, without any indication that he was offered a chance to deny the resurrection.` },
    { h: "James's death", t: `Josephus (*Antiquities* 20.200) reports that James, "the brother of Jesus who was called Christ," was stoned in 62 CE by the high priest Ananus for "breaking the law." That's evidence of his execution, but it doesn't say he was killed for claiming to have seen the risen Jesus.` },
    { h: "Sincere martyrs for false beliefs", t: `Members of the Heaven's Gate group (1997) and the Peoples Temple (Jonestown, 1978) died for their beliefs. Buddhist monks have burned themselves to death in protest (Thích Quảng Đức, 1963). Countless Muslims, Hindus, Sikhs, and Jews have died rather than renounce their faiths. Joseph Smith died at Carthage Jail in 1844, and the Book of Mormon's witnesses never recanted. Early Christians would have regarded many of these as false religions.` },
    { h: "First-hand vs. secondhand belief", t: `The apologetic says the apostles "were in a position to know." But if they had visionary experiences, they *were* in a position to sincerely believe and still be wrong, just as people at Fatima were in a position to see the sun and reported it moving. And the later Christians who died in persecutions weren't eyewitnesses at all. They died for what they were told.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "People die for lies they think are true, but not for lies they know are false.", a: `Agreed, and no historian claims the apostles were knowingly lying. They believed what they'd experienced. That's the point: sincere conviction from a powerful religious experience is compatible with the experience having a natural cause. Martyrdom confirms the conviction, not its cause.`, k: "die for a lie know is false no one would die liars apostles" },
    { q: "All the apostles except John were martyred.", a: `That's a tradition, not a historical finding. The sources for most of the apostles' deaths are legendary texts written centuries later. Sean McDowell, who wrote his PhD on this question and is a Christian apologist, concludes that only a few have strong historical evidence.`, k: "all apostles martyred except john tradition" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "McDowell, S. (2015). The Fate of the Apostles. Ashgate (rev. ed. Routledge, 2024).",
    "Moss, C. (2013). The Myth of Persecution. HarperOne.",
    "Josephus. Antiquities 20.200.",
    "1 Clement 5; Acts 12:2.",
    "Bremmer, J. (ed.) (1996–2010). Studies on the Apocryphal Acts of the Apostles (series). Peeters.",
    "Hall, J. R. (2000). Apocalypse Observed: Religious Movements and Violence. Routledge."
  ],
  keys: "apostles died martyrs martyrdom die for a lie no one dies for a lie disciples killed tortured sincere would they die for something false",
  rel: ["minimal-facts", "appearances", "christianity-growth"]
},
{
  id: "paul-conversion",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "Paul's Conversion",
  who: "Habermas & Licona; William Lane Craig; Acts 9, 22, 26",
  claim: `Paul was a zealous Pharisee who persecuted Christians. Something radical changed him into Christianity's greatest missionary, who suffered beatings, imprisonment, and death for his faith. He said the risen Jesus appeared to him. A hostile witness converted by an appearance is powerful evidence.`,
  short: `Paul is our only first-hand witness to a resurrection appearance, and what he describes is a vision: God "revealed his Son in me" (Galatians 1:16). By his own account he was given to visions and ecstatic experiences (2 Corinthians 12). The three accounts of his conversion in Acts contradict each other in detail. Dramatic conversions from one side to the other happen in every religion. Paul's case shows that powerful religious experiences can transform people, not that the risen Jesus appeared.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Paul's experience was a vision", t: `Paul never describes meeting a physical body. He says God "was pleased to reveal his Son in me" (Galatians 1:15–16) and that he received his gospel "through a revelation of Jesus Christ" (1:12). In Acts, what happens on the road is a blinding light and a voice; his companions see no one (9:7). Paul himself calls it a "heavenly vision" (Acts 26:19). He lists it alongside the other appearances using the same verb (1 Corinthians 15:8).` },
    { h: "A visionary personality", t: `Paul describes being "caught up into the third heaven" and into paradise, where he heard "things that are not to be told" (2 Corinthians 12:2–4), and refers to "visions and revelations of the Lord" (12:1). He speaks in tongues "more than all of you" (1 Corinthians 14:18). Someone with this kind of experience is especially likely to have a vision at a moment of crisis.` },
    { h: "Acts contradicts itself", t: `In Acts 9:7, Paul's companions "heard the voice but saw no one." In 22:9, they "saw the light but did not hear the voice." In 9:7, they "stood speechless"; in 26:14, "we had all fallen to the ground." In 9 and 22, Jesus tells Paul he'll be told what to do in Damascus; in 26:16–18, Jesus gives him his mission on the road. These differences suggest that Luke shaped the story for different speeches.` },
    { h: "Conversions from opponents are common", t: `People convert from hostility to faith, and from faith to hostility, in every religious tradition. Former Christians become Muslims, former Muslims become Christians, and evangelical pastors become atheists. Psychologists of religion describe sudden conversions as often following a period of inner conflict (William James, *The Varieties of Religious Experience*, 1902). Paul, persecuting people whose devotion he witnessed, may have had exactly that conflict.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Why would a persecutor convert unless he really saw Jesus?", a: `He believed he saw Jesus, in a vision, as he says. The question is what caused the vision. Persecutors are often deeply engaged with what they oppose, and inner conflict is a recognized precursor of sudden conversion. The same reasoning would validate Muhammad's visions or Joseph Smith's.`, k: "persecutor convert why unless really saw jesus hostile witness" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Segal, A. (1990). Paul the Convert. Yale University Press.",
    "James, W. (1902). The Varieties of Religious Experience, lectures IX–X.",
    "Lüdemann, G. (2004). The Resurrection of Christ, ch. on Paul.",
    "Fredriksen, P. (2017). Paul: The Pagans' Apostle. Yale University Press."
  ],
  keys: "paul conversion damascus road persecutor saul converted vision hostile witness paul saw jesus apostle paul",
  rel: ["appearances", "james-conversion", "minimal-facts"]
},
{
  id: "james-conversion",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "James, the Skeptical Brother, Converted",
  who: "Habermas & Licona; William Lane Craig",
  claim: `Jesus' brother James didn't believe in him during his ministry (Mark 3:21; John 7:5), yet afterward he became a leader of the Jerusalem church and died as a martyr. What could turn a skeptical brother into a believer willing to die? Paul says Jesus appeared to James (1 Corinthians 15:7).`,
  short: `The evidence that James was a skeptic is thin: a narrative in Mark that may reflect Mark's own polemic, and one line in John. Nothing says he was converted *by* an appearance; Paul's list says only that Jesus "appeared to James." Family members of a movement's founder often become leaders after the founder's death, and the early Jerusalem church was led by Jesus' relatives. James's prominence is explained by family ties at least as well as by a miracle.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Was James a skeptic?", t: `Mark 3:21 says Jesus' family went to restrain him because people said he was out of his mind, and Mark 3:31–35 has Jesus distinguish his true family from his relatives. John 7:5 says "not even his brothers believed in him." Some scholars think Mark's negative portrait of Jesus' family reflects later tensions between Mark's community and the Jerusalem church led by James (Crossan; Painter, *Just James*, 1997). Acts 1:14 places Jesus' brothers with the disciples right after the crucifixion, with no conversion story.` },
    { h: "No conversion account", t: `1 Corinthians 15:7 says Jesus "appeared to James." It doesn't say James was a skeptic before, or that the appearance converted him. A fragment of the Gospel of the Hebrews (in Jerome, *On Illustrious Men* 2) has James at the Last Supper vowing not to eat until he sees Jesus risen, which implies he was already a follower.` },
    { h: "Dynastic leadership", t: `After Jesus' death, leadership in Jerusalem went to his brother James, and later, according to Eusebius (*Ecclesiastical History* 3.11; 3.20), to other relatives of Jesus (the *desposynoi*, "those belonging to the Lord"). This pattern, where a founder's family assumes leadership, is common in religious movements (compare the role of Muhammad's family in Shi'a Islam). A brother who became a leader needs no miracle to explain him.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "James died as a martyr for his belief in the resurrection.", a: `Josephus says James was stoned in 62 CE for "breaking the law," at the instigation of the high priest Ananus. He doesn't say James was killed for believing in the resurrection, or that he was given a chance to recant. And dying for a sincere belief shows it was sincere, not that it was true (see [[martyrdom]]).`, k: "james martyr died resurrection belief stoned josephus" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Painter, J. (1997). Just James: The Brother of Jesus in History and Tradition. University of South Carolina Press.",
    "Eisenman, R. (1997). James the Brother of Jesus. Viking.",
    "Eusebius. Ecclesiastical History 2.23; 3.11; 3.20.",
    "Jerome. On Illustrious Men 2.",
    "Josephus. Antiquities 20.200."
  ],
  keys: "james brother of jesus skeptic converted unbelieving brother appeared to james leader jerusalem church",
  rel: ["paul-conversion", "martyrdom", "minimal-facts"]
},
{
  id: "women-witnesses",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "Women Discovered the Tomb (the Criterion of Embarrassment)",
  who: "William Lane Craig; N. T. Wright; Habermas & Licona",
  claim: `In 1st-century Judaism, women's testimony was regarded as nearly worthless (Josephus, *Antiquities* 4.219). If the Gospel writers had invented the empty tomb story, they would have used men, such as Peter and John, as the discoverers. The fact that women are the first witnesses is embarrassing, and so it must be historical.`,
  short: `The "criterion of embarrassment" has been sharply criticized in recent scholarship. What looks embarrassing to us may not have been to the authors, and stories can include awkward elements for literary reasons. In Mark, the male disciples have fled, so only the women are left to witness the burial and the tomb, which fits Mark's theme of the disciples' failure. Women's testimony was not uniformly rejected in Jewish law. And the earliest list of witnesses, in Paul, leaves the women out entirely.`,
  points: [
    { h: "The criteria approach has collapsed", t: `Chris Keith and Anthony Le Donne (eds.), *Jesus, Criteria, and the Demise of Authenticity* (2012), argue that the traditional criteria for authenticity, including embarrassment, are unreliable tools. Authors include material for their own reasons, and what was embarrassing depends on the audience. Even if an element was embarrassing, that shows the author had a reason to keep it, not that it happened.` },
    { h: "Mark needed the women", t: `In Mark, all the male disciples desert Jesus at his arrest (14:50). Peter denies him (14:66–72). Only the women watch the crucifixion "from a distance" (15:40) and see where he's buried (15:47). The narrative logic requires them to discover the tomb, because no one else is left. That fits Mark's theme that the insiders fail and outsiders are faithful.` },
    { h: "Women weren't worthless witnesses", t: `Josephus does say women's testimony wasn't accepted (*Antiquities* 4.219), but rabbinic law accepted women's testimony in some matters, such as testifying to a husband's death so that a widow could remarry (Mishnah Yevamot 16:7). The Gospels aren't legal proceedings; they're stories for communities in which women played prominent roles (Romans 16 names many women leaders).` },
    { h: "Paul leaves them out", t: `The earliest list of resurrection witnesses (1 Corinthians 15:5–8) begins with Cephas (Peter) and doesn't mention any women or the tomb. This suggests that the tradition of women discovering the tomb wasn't part of the earliest preaching, which is consistent with it developing later, with Mark (see [[empty-tomb]]).` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "No one would invent women as witnesses in that culture.", a: `Mark didn't need to invent them for evidential purposes. He needed someone to find the tomb after the men had all fled, and women at a tomb on the third day to anoint a body is a natural narrative detail. Celsus mocked the story in the 2nd century, but that shows how later pagans reacted, not how Mark's community saw it. And "they wouldn't have invented it" isn't evidence that a miracle happened.`, k: "no one would invent women witnesses culture testimony worthless" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Keith, C. & Le Donne, A. (eds.) (2012). Jesus, Criteria, and the Demise of Authenticity. T&T Clark.",
    "Josephus. Antiquities 4.219; Mishnah Yevamot 16:7.",
    "Origen. Against Celsus 2.55.",
    "Bauckham, R. (2002). Gospel Women. Eerdmans.",
    "Ehrman, B. (2016). Jesus Before the Gospels. HarperOne."
  ],
  keys: "women witnesses criterion of embarrassment women found tomb testimony of women not accepted invented men mary magdalene",
  rel: ["empty-tomb", "minimal-facts", "legendary-growth"]
},
{
  id: "creed-1cor15",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "The Early Creed of 1 Corinthians 15",
  who: "Gary Habermas; William Lane Craig; James D. G. Dunn ('within months')",
  claim: `In 1 Corinthians 15:3–7, Paul passes on a creed he "received," which scholars date to within a few years of the crucifixion, perhaps from his visit to Peter and James in Jerusalem around 35 CE (Galatians 1:18–19). Belief in the death, burial, resurrection, and appearances of Jesus therefore goes back to the very beginning. There wasn't time for legend to develop.`,
  short: `An early date for the creed is plausible, and skeptics can grant it. But it shows only that the *belief* in appearances arose very early, which is what a grief-vision explanation predicts, since visions would happen soon after the death. The creed doesn't mention an empty tomb, women, physical contact, Jerusalem, or Galilee. It says the resurrection was "according to the scriptures," which means it was understood through scripture, not described as a historical report. Early belief isn't the same as true belief.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Early belief is predicted by the visions explanation", t: `If the disciples had visions in the days and weeks after the crucifixion, belief in the resurrection would be early. The creed's date confirms the timing of the belief. It doesn't distinguish between explanations of what caused it.` },
    { h: "What the creed doesn't say", t: `It says Jesus died, was buried, was raised on the third day, and appeared (*ōphthē*) to various people. It says nothing about the tomb being found empty, about women, about where the appearances happened, or about what was seen. It's exactly as compatible with visionary experiences as with a physical body.` },
    { h: "'According to the scriptures'", t: `"Raised on the third day in accordance with the scriptures" points to a scriptural basis, probably Hosea 6:2 ("on the third day he will raise us up"), and to the idea of vindication through suffering. The creed presents the resurrection as an interpretation of scripture. "The third day" may reflect theology as much as chronology.` },
    { h: "Fast legends", t: `Legendary claims can arise very quickly in religious movements. Within weeks of the death of the Lubavitcher Rebbe, Menachem Mendel Schneerson, in 1994, some followers reported sightings and maintained that he wasn't really dead (Dein, 2011). Followers of Sabbatai Zevi produced miracle stories during his lifetime. Early belief is not protected from error.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "The creed is within 3–5 years of the crucifixion. That's too early for legend.", a: `I can grant the date. But visionary experiences don't take years to develop: they happen in days and weeks, which is why the belief would be early. The creed reports appearances in the most minimal terms, without the physical details that later Gospels add. And legends can form within weeks, as with the Lubavitcher Rebbe's followers in 1994.`, k: "creed 3 5 years crucifixion too early legend early date" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Dunn, J. D. G. (2003). Jesus Remembered. Eerdmans.",
    "Conzelmann, H. (1975). 1 Corinthians. Hermeneia. Fortress.",
    "Allison, D. C. (2005). Resurrecting Jesus. T&T Clark.",
    "Dein, S. (2011). Lubavitcher Messianism: What Really Happens When Prophecy Fails? Continuum."
  ],
  keys: "1 corinthians 15 creed early creed received within years of crucifixion too early for legend paul jerusalem peter james",
  rel: ["appearances", "five-hundred", "legendary-growth", "dying-messiah"]
},
{
  id: "legendary-growth",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "The Resurrection Stories Are Too Early and Sober for Legend",
  who: "A. N. Sherwin-White, Roman Society and Roman Law in the New Testament (1963); William Lane Craig; Lee Strobel",
  claim: `Historian A. N. Sherwin-White showed that even two generations aren't enough for legend to wipe out a historical core. The Gospel accounts are restrained compared with later apocryphal gospels: no description of the resurrection itself, no fantastic details. That sobriety marks them as historical.`,
  short: `The four canonical accounts show clear signs of growth over time. Mark has no appearances and an ending in silence. Matthew adds an earthquake, a descending angel, guards, and dead saints walking into Jerusalem. Luke adds a physical Jesus who eats fish and ascends. John adds Thomas touching the wounds. Later, the Gospel of Peter adds giant angels and a talking cross. Legend didn't wipe out a historical core: it grew around one, the crucifixion and the disciples' experiences, which is what the critical view says. Sherwin-White's comment concerned the persistence of a historical core, not whether miracle claims can develop.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Watching the story grow", t: `**Mark** (c. 70): a young man in white at the empty tomb; the women flee and tell no one; no appearances. **Matthew** (c. 80–90): an earthquake; an angel descends, rolls back the stone, and sits on it; guards faint; Jesus meets the women; the eleven meet him on a mountain in Galilee; "some doubted." **Luke** (c. 85–95): two men in dazzling clothes; appearances in and near Jerusalem only; Jesus eats fish and has "flesh and bones"; he ascends from Bethany. **John** (c. 90–110): Mary Magdalene meets Jesus in the garden; Thomas is invited to touch the wounds; a further Galilee appearance in an appendix (ch. 21). **Gospel of Peter** (2nd century): angels as tall as the sky, Jesus even taller, followed by a cross that speaks.` },
    { h: "Matthew's walking dead", t: `Matthew 27:51–53 says that when Jesus died, the earth shook, tombs opened, and "many bodies of the saints who had fallen asleep were raised... and they entered the holy city and appeared to many." No other Gospel, no other Christian writer of the period, and no Jewish or Roman source mentions this. When evangelical scholar Mike Licona suggested in 2010 that the passage might be apocalyptic imagery rather than history, he was sharply criticized by inerrantists such as Norman Geisler and Albert Mohler. The passage is either legend in a Gospel, or an event so public it's inexplicable that no one recorded it.` },
    { h: "When the ascension happened", t: `Luke 24:50–51 places the ascension on Easter Sunday evening, in Bethany. Acts 1:3–9, by the same author, places it 40 days later, on the Mount of Olives. The author's own story changed between his two books.` },
    { h: "What Sherwin-White said", t: `Sherwin-White, a Roman historian, argued that the historical core of events in the Gospels and Acts, such as legal procedures and settings, is plausible, and that legend would need time to overwhelm it. That's compatible with the critical view: a historical core (Jesus' crucifixion; his followers' experiences) surrounded by growing legendary development. Sherwin-White didn't argue that miracles were historical, and in Roman history he also treated miracle stories in sources like Tacitus as legends.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "The Gospel accounts are sober. They don't describe the resurrection itself, unlike legendary texts.", a: `Matthew's account includes an earthquake, an angel like lightning, fainting guards, and an unrecorded mass resurrection of the dead in Jerusalem. That's not sober. The trend across the Gospels is toward more spectacle and more physical proof. Their relative restraint compared to the Gospel of Peter shows only that they're earlier in the same development.`, k: "sober accounts restrained don't describe resurrection itself apocryphal gospel of peter" },
    { q: "Two generations isn't enough time for legend to develop (Sherwin-White).", a: `Legends form much faster than that. The Lubavitcher Rebbe's followers reported his appearances within weeks of his death. Stories about Sabbatai Zevi's miracles spread across Europe within months. Sherwin-White's point was that a historical core survives, not that miracle stories can't arise. The core here, a crucified preacher and followers who had visions, is what critical scholars accept.`, k: "two generations sherwin white not enough time legend develop" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Sherwin-White, A. N. (1963). Roman Society and Roman Law in the New Testament. Oxford University Press.",
    "Allison, D. C. (2021). The Resurrection of Jesus. T&T Clark.",
    "Licona, M. (2010). The Resurrection of Jesus: A New Historiographical Approach. InterVarsity.",
    "Ehrman, B. (2003). Lost Christianities. Oxford University Press (on the Gospel of Peter).",
    "Dein, S. (2011). Lubavitcher Messianism. Continuum.",
    "Scholem, G. (1973). Sabbatai Sevi. Princeton University Press."
  ],
  keys: "legendary development legend growth resurrection accounts sober sherwin white two generations matthew 27 saints raised zombies gospel of peter ascension",
  rel: ["contradictions", "empty-tomb", "appearances", "creed-1cor15"]
},
{
  id: "bayes-resurrection",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "Bayesian Analysis Supports the Resurrection",
  who: "Richard Swinburne, The Resurrection of God Incarnate (2003); Timothy & Lydia McGrew, 'The Argument from Miracles' (Blackwell Companion, 2009)",
  claim: `Applying Bayes' theorem rigorously, the evidence for the resurrection is overwhelming. Swinburne calculates a probability of about 97% that Jesus rose. The McGrews argue that the testimony of the disciples, Paul, and the women produces a Bayes factor of around 10^44 in favor of resurrection, enough to overwhelm even a very low prior probability.`,
  short: `Bayesian analysis is the right framework, but these calculations depend on inputs that are assumed rather than shown. Swinburne's 97% comes from a high prior that God exists and would want to become incarnate, which is the question at issue. The McGrews' enormous Bayes factor treats each piece of testimony as independent and assumes that natural explanations make the testimony extremely unlikely. That ignores known mechanisms such as grief visions, social contagion, and legendary growth, which make that kind of testimony quite likely even if no resurrection occurred.`,
  points: [
    { h: "The prior", t: `The prior probability of a resurrection should reflect background knowledge: the dead don't return to life, while reports of miraculous events and appearances of the dead are common in every culture and era. Swinburne raises the prior by first arguing that God probably exists and would probably become incarnate and rise to authenticate his message. Each of those steps is disputed (see [[cumulative-case]]). Without them, the prior is extremely low.` },
    { h: "Independence is assumed", t: `Multiplying likelihood ratios for each witness requires the witnesses to be independent. The resurrection witnesses were members of a small, close group who shared beliefs, expectations, and conversations. The reports we have were passed through the same community and edited by the same authors. Correlated witnesses add far less evidence than independent ones, and the McGrews' figure comes largely from treating them as independent.` },
    { h: "The likelihood of the evidence on naturalism", t: `The key question is: given no resurrection, how likely is it that we'd have the evidence we have, which is texts by believers decades later reporting appearances and an empty tomb? Given what we know about grief, religious experience, and legend formation, that's not very unlikely. Similar evidence exists for many events Christians reject: appearances of Apollonius, Joseph Smith's golden plates, Sathya Sai Baba's miracles.` },
    { h: "Critical work in philosophy", t: `Robert Cavin ("Is There Sufficient Historical Evidence to Establish the Resurrection of Jesus?", *Faith and Philosophy*, 1995) argues the evidence is insufficient even for the claim that Jesus' body was physically raised, as opposed to other hypotheses. Herman Philipse (*God in the Age of Science?*, 2012) gives a detailed Bayesian critique of Swinburne. John Earman, though critical of Hume's argument, notes that ordinary testimony can only overcome very low priors if it is truly independent and reliable, which ancient religious testimony isn't.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "You're assuming naturalism by giving resurrection a low prior.", a: `No. A low prior follows from background evidence that people don't come back from the dead, which theists accept too, or they'd believe every report of a resurrection. Even with God in the picture, the prior that God raised *this* person, rather than any of the countless others whose followers reported miracles, needs independent support.`, k: "assuming naturalism low prior bias anti supernatural presupposition" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Swinburne, R. (2003). The Resurrection of God Incarnate. Oxford University Press.",
    "McGrew, T. & McGrew, L. (2009). 'The Argument from Miracles: A Cumulative Case for the Resurrection of Jesus of Nazareth.' In Craig & Moreland (eds.), The Blackwell Companion to Natural Theology.",
    "Cavin, R. G. (1995). 'Is There Sufficient Historical Evidence to Establish the Resurrection of Jesus?' Faith and Philosophy 12(3): 361–379.",
    "Philipse, H. (2012). God in the Age of Science? Oxford University Press, Part III.",
    "Earman, J. (2000). Hume's Abject Failure: The Argument Against Miracles. Oxford University Press.",
    "Hájek, A. & Hartmann, S. (2010). 'Bayesian Epistemology.' In Dancy, Sosa & Steup (eds.), A Companion to Epistemology. Wiley-Blackwell."
  ],
  keys: "bayes theorem resurrection probability swinburne 97 percent mcgrew bayes factor 10^44 prior probability likelihood",
  rel: ["miracles-hume", "minimal-facts", "cumulative-case"]
},
{
  id: "alternative-theories",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "Every Naturalistic Theory Has Been Refuted",
  who: "William Lane Craig; Gary Habermas; Lee Strobel, The Case for Easter (2003)",
  claim: `Skeptics have proposed many theories: Jesus didn't die (swoon), the disciples stole the body, the women went to the wrong tomb, the disciples hallucinated. Each fails. Roman executioners didn't botch crucifixions; the disciples wouldn't die for a lie; someone would have checked the right tomb; hallucinations don't happen to groups or explain the empty tomb. Only the resurrection survives.`,
  short: `Refuting the weakest versions of individual theories isn't the same as refuting naturalism. The serious naturalistic account combines grief visions, the dynamics of religious groups, scriptural interpretation, and legendary development, and it isn't touched by these objections. And even if no natural explanation were available, the right conclusion would be "we don't know what happened," which is far more probable than "a miracle happened." We don't conclude that a magician has real powers because we can't work out the trick.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Straw men", t: `Few critical scholars hold the swoon or conspiracy theories. The view of most non-Christian scholars, and of some Christian ones, is a combination: Jesus died; his followers had visionary experiences that they interpreted as appearances; the belief spread through the group; the empty-tomb narrative developed or was interpreted within that belief. Knocking down the swoon theory has no bearing on that view.` },
    { h: "The combination isn't ad hoc", t: `Each element is independently known: bereavement experiences are common; group religious experiences happen; new movements reinterpret their founders' deaths (Festinger's *When Prophecy Fails*, 1956; Dein, 2011); and stories grow in retelling (see [[legendary-growth]]). A combination of known processes is more probable than a single process with no known instance.` },
    { h: "Ignorance doesn't favor miracles", t: `Suppose we couldn't explain the evidence at all. We have little reliable information about events 2,000 years ago, from sources written decades later by believers. Gaps are inevitable. "We don't know" is always available and almost always more probable than a supernatural intervention. Many events in history remain unexplained without anyone inferring a miracle.` },
    { h: "Consistency test", t: `Apologists reject the resurrection reports of other traditions (Apollonius of Tyana's post-mortem appearance; Sabbatai Zevi's followers; the Lubavitcher Rebbe's sightings) without having refuted every alternative explanation. The same standard applied to Jesus gives the same result.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "The disciples couldn't all have hallucinated, so the hallucination theory fails.", a: `The serious proposal isn't that each disciple independently hallucinated. It's that some had visions (probably Peter first), others had experiences shaped by expectation and group dynamics, and the group interpreted them together. That's how collective religious experiences typically work. And our sources for "group appearances" are summary lists and later Gospels, not first-hand descriptions.`, k: "couldn't all hallucinate hallucination theory fails individual" },
    { q: "Roman soldiers were experts at killing, so Jesus couldn't have survived (swoon theory).", a: `Agreed, he almost certainly died. I'm not proposing the swoon theory, and few scholars do. Refuting it doesn't help the case for resurrection.`, k: "swoon theory roman soldiers survived crucifixion didn't die" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Festinger, L., Riecken, H. & Schachter, S. (1956). When Prophecy Fails. University of Minnesota Press.",
    "Dein, S. (2011). Lubavitcher Messianism. Continuum.",
    "Komarnitsky, K. (2014). Doubting Jesus' Resurrection, 2nd ed.",
    "Allison, D. C. (2021). The Resurrection of Jesus.",
    "Martin, M. (1991). The Case Against Christianity. Temple University Press, ch. 3."
  ],
  keys: "naturalistic theories refuted swoon theory stolen body wrong tomb hallucination theory conspiracy alternative explanations fail resurrection only explanation",
  rel: ["minimal-facts", "appearances", "dying-messiah"]
},
{
  id: "dying-messiah",
  cat: "resurrection",
  title: "Jews Would Never Invent a Crucified, Risen Messiah",
  who: "N. T. Wright, The Resurrection of the Son of God (2003); William Lane Craig",
  claim: `First-century Jews expected a triumphant messiah, not a crucified one; a crucified man was cursed (Deuteronomy 21:23). And they expected resurrection only for everyone at the end of time, not for one person in the middle of history. So the disciples' belief that the crucified Jesus had been raised was so unexpected that only a real resurrection can explain it.`,
  short: `The disciples didn't invent the idea from nothing. They had to make sense of a devastating failure, and religious groups in that situation reliably reinterpret rather than abandon their beliefs. The materials for the reinterpretation were at hand: the vindication of righteous martyrs (2 Maccabees 7; Wisdom 2–5), an expected general resurrection (Daniel 12:2), and even popular speculation that John the Baptist had been raised (Mark 6:14–16). "This was unexpected, so it must be true" isn't a valid inference.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Cognitive dissonance and failed prophecy", t: `Leon Festinger's classic study (*When Prophecy Fails*, 1956) found that when a group's central prediction fails, committed members often reinterpret the failure and proselytize more. Later examples: after Sabbatai Zevi converted to Islam in 1666, many followers interpreted it as a deliberate mystical act. After the Lubavitcher Rebbe died in 1994, some followers said he hadn't really died, or would return as messiah (Dein, 2011). The disciples' situation, a messiah executed, is a textbook trigger.` },
    { h: "Martyr vindication was a known idea", t: `Jewish texts from before Jesus describe God vindicating righteous people who die for their faith. In 2 Maccabees 7, martyred brothers expect God to raise them. In Wisdom of Solomon 2–5, the righteous man is mocked and killed by the wicked, then exalted, to their astonishment. Daniel 12:2–3 describes a resurrection in which "those who lead many to righteousness" shine like stars. Applying these ideas to a martyred teacher was a natural move.` },
    { h: "Raised prophets were imaginable", t: `Mark 6:14–16 reports that some people, including Herod Antipas, said that "John the baptizer has been raised from the dead" and that this explained Jesus' powers. That's a report, in our earliest Gospel, of 1st-century Jews thinking a recently executed prophet had been raised, *before* Easter. Revelation 11:11 describes two martyred witnesses raised after three and a half days. Elijah and Enoch were believed to have been taken up bodily.` },
    { h: "The curse was reinterpreted", t: `Paul confronts Deuteronomy 21:23 directly: "Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law by becoming a curse for us" (Galatians 3:13). That's the kind of creative reinterpretation that dissonance reduction produces: the apparent refutation becomes the central saving event.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "No Jew expected one person to rise before the general resurrection. The idea was unprecedented.", a: `Mark 6:14–16 says people thought John the Baptist had been raised and was working through Jesus. That's a pre-Easter belief in a single individual's resurrection. And "unprecedented" ideas arise in new religious movements all the time. The novelty of a belief is not evidence that it's true.`, k: "no jew expected one person rise before general resurrection unprecedented novel" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Festinger, L., Riecken, H. & Schachter, S. (1956). When Prophecy Fails. University of Minnesota Press.",
    "Dein, S. (2011). Lubavitcher Messianism: What Really Happens When Prophecy Fails? Continuum.",
    "Scholem, G. (1973). Sabbatai Sevi: The Mystical Messiah. Princeton University Press.",
    "Nickelsburg, G. (2006). Resurrection, Immortality, and Eternal Life in Intertestamental Judaism, expanded ed. Harvard University Press.",
    "Wright, N. T. (2003). The Resurrection of the Son of God. Fortress."
  ],
  keys: "jews wouldn't invent crucified messiah risen messiah unexpected cursed deuteronomy 21:23 general resurrection end of time cognitive dissonance when prophecy fails",
  rel: ["alternative-theories", "creed-1cor15", "messianic-prophecy"]
}
]);
